					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/harrisbaker" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2858460); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2858460&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608584955" alt="HarrisBaker" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2858460)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/harrisbaker" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2858460); return false;" >HarrisBaker</a></span> <span class="odate time_1481966900 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">17 Dec 2016 09:28</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2711056">
									

<p>I like it. The Secure Containment Initiative is a new one on me, and the description of the ritual is just majestic.<br />
I like the slightly antiquated language, too. It seems very precise in the words used, too, which also appeals to me.<br />
The ending has been called an anticlimax, but it fits with the set-up at the beginning. I think it's fine.<br />
I was wondering whether this was part of the Unfounded canon. The discussion seems to say otherwise, though. I think it would fit nicely, if the SCI was a version of the Foundation that eventually died out.</p>
<p>+1. Good show, I say sir!</p>

